.TH GIST 1 "0.2.0" "gist" "gist Manual"
.nh
.ad l
.SH "NAME"
gist \- indexed regex code search with ripgrep's grammar
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
\fBgist\fR [OPTIONS] PATTERN [PATH...]
.br
\fBgist\fR index [ROOT...]
.br
\fBgist\fR status [\-\-json]
.br
\fBgist\fR serve [ROOT...]
.br
\fBgist\fR codex build|count|tally|status [TEXT]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
gist searches the tree for PATTERN and prints the matching lines. It is a
byte\-for\-byte drop\-in for ripgrep's default behavior \[u2014] the same
flags, the same gitignore and hidden\-file precedence, the same 0/1/2 exit
codes \[u2014] riding a persisted trigram index that lets it skip reading
files no match can be in.
.sp
No setup is required. With no index, gist live\-scans and answers exactly what
a walk would; with a current index, it answers the same bytes faster by
eliding provably non\-candidate reads. Acceleration is never allowed to change
output, and \-\-no\-index forces the pure walk if you want to see that for
yourself.
.sp
Beyond parity, gist adds the shapes ripgrep cannot express: \-\-rank puts a
symbol's definition above its call sites and sinks generated files, \-P rides
a trigram\-prefiltered PCRE2 so lookaround and backreferences stay indexed,
and a resident session answers repeat queries without paying process startup.
.sp
The options below are grouped by what a flag CHANGES, not by spelling, because
that is the question a reader arrives with. The four sections are the same
reach classification the parser uses to decide what a persisted configuration
file is allowed to say.
.SH "COMMANDS"
.TP
\fBindex\fR
build and persist the trigram index over ROOT (default: this tree)
.TP
\fBstatus\fR
is an index ready, how fresh, how big
.TP
\fBserve\fR
run the resident warm session (auto\-spawned; run it to scope the roots)
.TP
\fBcodex\fR
exact corpus\-wide literal counts off the compressed self\-index
.sp
Sub-commands: build, count, tally, status
.TP
\fBsearch\fR
the ordinary search, addressed with a verb
.TP
\fBrg\fR
the same engine under the name an alias would give it
.SH "OPTIONS"
.SS "Corpus — which bytes are searched"
Which files, and which of their bytes, the engine is given to read.
.TP
\fB\-\-hidden\fR
search hidden files and directories
.TP
\fB\-a\fR, \fB\-\-text\fR
search binary files as if they were text
.TP
\fB\-\-binary\fR
search binary files and print every matching line
.sp
improvement: a code locator searches a NUL\-bearing file in full and prints
every matching line, rather than rg's opaque 'binary file matches' summary
that suppresses the lines and stops
.TP
\fB\-u\fR, \fB\-\-unrestricted\fR
filter less: \-u ignore rules, \-uu also hidden, \-uuu also binary
.sp
\-u=\-\-no\-ignore, \-uu adds hidden, \-uuu adds \-\-binary; the ignore/hidden
ladder is rg\-identical, \-uuu inherits the \-\-binary improvement
.TP
\fB\-L\fR, \fB\-\-follow\fR
follow symbolic links
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-follow\fR
do not follow symbolic links
.TP
\fB\-\-glob\-case\-insensitive\fR
match every \-\-glob case\-insensitively
.TP
\fB\-t\fR, \fB\-\-type\fR=\fITYPE\fR
search only files of this type
.sp
239 accepted values.
.TP
\fB\-T\fR, \fB\-\-type\-not\fR=\fITYPE\fR
skip files of this type
.sp
239 accepted values.
.TP
\fB\-g\fR, \fB\-\-glob\fR=\fIGLOB\fR
include paths matching this glob, or exclude with a leading !
.TP
\fB\-\-iglob\fR=\fIGLOB\fR
as \-\-glob, matched case\-insensitively
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-depth\fR, \fB\-\-maxdepth\fR=\fINUM\fR
descend at most this many directories
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-filesize\fR=\fISIZE\fR
skip files larger than this size
.TP
\fB\-\-type\-add\fR=\fITYPESPEC\fR
define or extend a file type, as name:glob
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-ignore\fR
obey no ignore file at all
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-ignore\-vcs\fR
obey no .gitignore rules
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-ignore\-dot\fR
obey no .ignore rules
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-ignore\-parent\fR
obey no ignore rules from parent directories
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-ignore\-exclude\fR
obey no .git/info/exclude rules
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-ignore\-files\fR
obey no \-\-ignore\-file source
.TP
\fB\-\-ignore\-files\fR
obey \-\-ignore\-file sources again
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-require\-git\fR
apply git ignore rules outside a repository too
.TP
\fB\-\-require\-git\fR
apply git ignore rules only inside a repository
.TP
\fB\-\-ignore\-file\-case\-insensitive\fR
match ignore\-file globs case\-insensitively
.TP
\fB\-\-ignore\-file\fR=\fIFILE\fR
read more ignore rules from this file
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-ignore\-global\fR
obey no global git excludesFile
.sp
git core.excludesFile ($HOME/.gitconfig or $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/git/config
\[u2192] default $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/git/ignore) is read by default in a repo;
this disables that global tier only
.TP
\fB\-\-one\-file\-system\fR
do not descend across a filesystem boundary
.TP
\fB\-z\fR, \fB\-\-search\-zip\fR
search inside compressed files
.sp
improvement: identical results to rg across every codec, but gzip/zlib/zstd/xz
decode IN\-PROCESS (no per\-file `gzip \-dc` fork); bzip2/lz4/brotli/lzma/.Z
shell the standard tool exactly as rg does
.TP
\fB\-\-pre\fR=\fICOMMAND\fR
filter every file through this command before searching it
.sp
rg parity: the command receives the file path as argv[1] AND the file's bytes
on stdin; a non\-zero exit is an error (exit 2)
.TP
\fB\-\-pre\-glob\fR=\fIGLOB\fR
run \-\-pre only on the paths matching this glob
.TP
\fB\-E\fR, \fB\-\-encoding\fR=\fIENCODING\fR
decode input with this character encoding
.sp
233 accepted values.
.sp
auto/none + the full WHATWG label table (rg's encoding_rs set): UTF\-8/16, the
single\-byte pages, and CJK gb18030/GBK, Big5, EUC\-JP, Shift_JIS, EUC\-KR,
ISO\-2022\-JP; an unrecognized label fails loud
.SS "Semantics — what counts as a match"
Given those bytes, which lines match. These are the flags that can change your
answer.
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-ignore\-case\fR
match case\-insensitively
.sp
Unicode case folding by default (full simple case\-fold orbits);
(?\-u)/\-\-no\-unicode selects ASCII folding
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-case\-sensitive\fR
match case\-sensitively
.TP
\fB\-S\fR, \fB\-\-smart\-case\fR
match case\-insensitively unless the pattern has uppercase
.sp
codepoint\-aware uppercase detection and Unicode folding by default;
(?\-u)/\-\-no\-unicode selects ASCII
.TP
\fB\-w\fR, \fB\-\-word\-regexp\fR
require a word boundary on both sides of a match
.sp
\-w and regex \eb/\ew use Unicode word characters by default (rg parity);
(?\-u)/\-\-no\-unicode selects ASCII byte word chars
.TP
\fB\-F\fR, \fB\-\-fixed\-strings\fR
read the pattern as literal bytes, not a regex
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-invert\-match\fR
print the lines that do not match
.TP
\fB\-\-null\-data\fR
treat NUL, not newline, as the line terminator
.TP
\fB\-x\fR, \fB\-\-line\-regexp\fR
require the pattern to match a whole line
.TP
\fB\-\-stop\-on\-nonmatch\fR
stop a file at its first non\-matching line
.TP
\fB\-\-crlf\fR
treat CRLF as the line terminator
.TP
\fB\-U\fR, \fB\-\-multiline\fR
let one match span line boundaries
.TP
\fB\-\-multiline\-dotall\fR
multiline, and let . match a newline too
.TP
\fB\-m\fR, \fB\-\-max\-count\fR=\fINUM\fR
stop after this many matches in each file
.TP
\fB\-e\fR, \fB\-\-regexp\fR=\fIPATTERN\fR
add a pattern; repeatable, and safe for one starting with a dash
.TP
\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-file\fR=\fIFILE\fR
read patterns from a file, one per line
.TP
\fB\-\-in\-comments\fR
keep only the matches that fall inside a comment
.sp
keep only matches whose span begins inside a //, #, or /* */ comment (native
span\-lexed view; mutually exclusive with \-\-in\-code)
.TP
\fB\-\-in\-code\fR
keep only the matches that fall outside every comment
.sp
keep only matches OUTSIDE any comment (native span\-lexed view; mutually
exclusive with \-\-in\-comments)
.TP
\fB\-\-unicode\fR
Unicode mode: codepoint classes and full case folding
.sp
linear\-engine Unicode mode (rg default): full case\-fold orbits, codepoint
\ew/\ed/\es/./\ep{\[u2026]}, and Unicode \eb/\eB/\e<\e>/\-w
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-unicode\fR
byte mode: single\-byte classes and ASCII word boundaries
.sp
byte/ASCII mode: single\-byte classes and ASCII \ew/\eb (equivalent to a
leading (?\-u))
.TP
\fB\-\-engine\fR=\fIENGINE\fR
which match backend to compile the pattern with
.RS
.TP
\fBdefault\fR
the linear engine \[u2014] no backtracking, no catastrophic case
.TP
\fBpcre2\fR
the vendored PCRE2 JIT \[u2014] lookaround and backreferences
.TP
\fBauto\fR
linear, escalating to PCRE2 only when the pattern needs it
.RE
.sp
default/auto/pcre2 select the engine exactly as rg: default = linear RE2/Pike,
auto escalates to PCRE2 only for lookaround/backreferences the linear engine
declines, pcre2 selects PCRE2 outright (the gist\-native \-\-rank is
linear\-only)
.TP
\fB\-\-pcre2\-unicode\fR
PCRE2 Unicode mode, under \-P or an escalated \-\-engine auto
.sp
PCRE2 UTF+UCP mode (rg's \-P default); as in rg it governs the PCRE2 backend,
effective under \-P/auto and inert under gist's extra linear default
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-pcre2\-unicode\fR
PCRE2 raw\-byte mode, under the same two backends
.sp
PCRE2 raw\-byte/ASCII mode; as in rg it governs the PCRE2 backend, effective
under \-P/auto and inert under gist's extra linear default
.TP
\fB\-P\fR, \fB\-\-pcre2\fR
use PCRE2: lookaround, backreferences, Unicode properties
.sp
improvement: the vendored PCRE2 JIT backend (lookaround, backreferences,
Unicode properties) yields rg's exact \-P match set, but trigram\-prefiltered
like the linear engine \[u2014] the only INDEXED PCRE search in the field (the
gist\-native \-\-rank is linear\-only)
.TP
\fB\-\-auto\-hybrid\-regex\fR
escalate to PCRE2 only when needed (rg's spelling of \-\-engine auto)
.sp
rg's own deprecated spelling of \-\-engine auto; escalates to PCRE2 only for a
pattern the linear engine declines
.SS "Presentation — how the answer is shown"
How the matches are written out. The match set itself is untouched.
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-only\-matching\fR
print only the matched part of each line
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-line\-number\fR
prefix each line with its line number
.TP
\fB\-N\fR, \fB\-\-no\-line\-number\fR
omit line numbers
.TP
\fB\-H\fR, \fB\-\-with\-filename\fR
prefix each line with its file path
.TP
\fB\-I\fR, \fB\-\-no\-filename\fR
omit file paths
.TP
\fB\-l\fR, \fB\-\-files\-with\-matches\fR
print only the paths that contain a match
.TP
\fB\-\-files\-without\-match\fR
print only the paths that contain no match
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-count\fR
print how many lines matched, per file
.TP
\fB\-\-count\-matches\fR
print how many matches there were, per file
.TP
\fB\-\-include\-zero\fR
in a count mode, also print the files with no match
.sp
in a count mode, print a path:0 line for every searched file with no match
(exit code unchanged)
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-include\-zero\fR
omit zero\-count files, the default
.TP
\fB\-\-column\fR
print the column of the first match on each line
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-column\fR
omit columns
.TP
\fB\-b\fR, \fB\-\-byte\-offset\fR
print the byte offset of each matching line
.TP
\fB\-\-vimgrep\fR
one line per match, in vim's quickfix shape
.TP
\fB\-\-heading\fR
group matches under their filename
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-heading\fR
prefix every line with its path instead of grouping
.TP
\fB\-\-trim\fR
strip leading whitespace from every printed line
.TP
\fB\-0\fR, \fB\-\-null\fR
terminate each printed path with NUL
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
print nothing and stop at the first match
.TP
\fB\-\-stats\fR
print search statistics after the results
.TP
\fB\-\-passthru\fR, \fB\-\-passthrough\fR
print every line, matching or not
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-columns\-preview\fR
show a truncated preview of an elided long line
.TP
\fB\-\-field\-match\-separator\fR=\fISEPARATOR\fR
the separator between a match line's fields
.TP
\fB\-\-field\-context\-separator\fR=\fISEPARATOR\fR
the separator between a context line's fields
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
emit results as JSON Lines
.TP
\fB\-\-files\fR
list every file that would be searched
.TP
\fB\-\-type\-list\fR
list every file type and the globs it covers
.sp
improvement: rg\-sorted, rg\-framed output over a strict SUPERSET of rg's type
registry (rg's rows byte\-identical; the rest richer, plus gist\-only types)
.TP
\fB\-\-sort\-files\fR
sort by path (rg's deprecated spelling of \-\-sort=path)
.sp
deprecated rg spelling of \-\-sort=path
.TP
\fB\-\-sort\fR=\fISORTKEY\fR
sort the results ascending by this key
.RS
.TP
\fBnone\fR
no ordering \[u2014] the fastest discovery order
.TP
\fBpath\fR
by display path
.TP
\fBmodified\fR
by mtime
.TP
\fBaccessed\fR
by atime
.TP
\fBcreated\fR
by birth time, falling back to ctime
.RE
.sp
improvement: path/modified/accessed/created, ascending; final ordering is
rg\-identical but produced over a parallel read (rg single\-threads sort), and
created falls back to ctime where the platform lacks birth time (rg cannot
sort at all)
.TP
\fB\-\-sortr\fR=\fISORTKEY\fR
sort the results descending by this key
.RS
.TP
\fBnone\fR
no ordering \[u2014] the fastest discovery order
.TP
\fBpath\fR
by display path
.TP
\fBmodified\fR
by mtime
.TP
\fBaccessed\fR
by atime
.TP
\fBcreated\fR
by birth time, falling back to ctime
.RE
.sp
improvement: as \-\-sort but descending; same rg\-identical ordering over a
parallel read and the same created\[u2192]ctime robustness fallback
.TP
\fB\-A\fR, \fB\-\-after\-context\fR=\fINUM\fR
print this many lines after each match
.TP
\fB\-B\fR, \fB\-\-before\-context\fR=\fINUM\fR
print this many lines before each match
.TP
\fB\-C\fR, \fB\-\-context\fR=\fINUM\fR
print this many lines on both sides of each match
.TP
\fB\-M\fR, \fB\-\-max\-columns\fR=\fINUM\fR
elide any line longer than this many columns
.TP
\fB\-\-path\-separator\fR=\fISEPARATOR\fR
the separator printed between path components
.TP
\fB\-\-context\-separator\fR=\fISEPARATOR\fR
the line printed between non\-adjacent context blocks
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-context\-separator\fR
print nothing between context blocks
.TP
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-\-replace\fR=\fITEXT\fR
print this text in place of each match
.TP
\fB\-\-color\fR=\fIWHEN\fR
when to colorize the output
.RS
.TP
\fBauto\fR
colorize only a terminal that has not opted out
.TP
\fBalways\fR
colorize regardless of destination
.TP
\fBnever\fR
never colorize
.TP
\fBansi\fR
always, and with ANSI escapes even on Windows
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-hyperlink\fR, \fB\-\-link\fR[=\fITARGET\fR]
make paths clickable: a posture, an editor alias, or a format
.sp
18 accepted values.
.sp
improvement: bare \-\-hyperlink forces links on;
\-\-hyperlink=auto|always|never sets the posture; \-\-hyperlink=<alias|format>
picks the destination AND turns links on. Default is auto (on for a
human\-shaped run in a terminal known to render OSC\-8, off otherwise) where
rg defaults to none, and gist never ties links to \-\-color/NO_COLOR.
GIST_HYPERLINK=<alias> is the standing\-preference spelling: it names the
destination but leaves the auto probe to decide whether
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-hyperlink\fR, \fB\-\-no\-link\fR
never emit hyperlinks
.TP
\fB\-\-hyperlink\-format\fR=\fITARGET\fR
the hyperlink template, or an editor alias
.sp
18 accepted values.
.sp
improvement: rg's spelling and grammar exactly
({path}/{line}/{column}/{host}/{wslprefix}, {{ }} escapes, same validation),
plus the aliases rg lacks (zed, windsurf, vscode\-remote, cursor\-remote) and
lexical path folding instead of a realpath(2) per matched file
.TP
\fB\-\-hostname\-bin\fR=\fICOMMAND\fR
a command whose output supplies {host} in a hyperlink
.sp
unset uses gethostname(2). Exists because inside WSL or a container the
kernel's own name is not the one a link has to reach
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-messages\fR
suppress per\-file errors, but not the exit code they set
.sp
suppress the per\-file stderr lane (unreadable path, symlink loop, \-\-pre
failure, 'no files were searched'); as in rg the exit code is unchanged, so a
silenced error still exits 2
.TP
\fB\-\-messages\fR
print per\-file errors
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-ignore\-messages\fR
suppress errors from unreadable ignore sources
.sp
suppress the narrower ignore\-source lane (an \-\-ignore\-file that will not
open); \-\-no\-messages subsumes it. gist's ignore parser accepts every line
rg's glob compiler would reject, so this lane carries unreadable sources
rather than rg's parse errors
.TP
\fB\-\-ignore\-messages\fR
print errors from unreadable ignore sources
.TP
\fB\-\-rank\fR[=\fIN\fR]
ranked view: definitions first, generated files demoted
.TP
\fB\-\-uncap\fR
lift the soft output budget for this query
.sp
lift the ~25k\-token soft output cap for this query; the hard OOM ceiling
still applies (GIST_UNCAP=1 is the env form)
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-stats\fR
print no search statistics
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-trim\fR
keep leading whitespace
.TP
\fB\-\-colors\fR=\fISPEC\fR
accepted for rg parity; the spec is validated, then discarded
.TP
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-pretty\fR
color, heading, and line numbers at once
.sp
rg's alias for \-\-color always \-\-heading \-\-line\-number; a later
\-N/\-\-no\-heading/\-\-color still wins
.TP
\fB\-\-plain\fR
print what a pipe would receive, on a terminal
.sp
the inverse pole: pin the answer to what a PIPE would receive even on a
terminal \[u2014] \-\-color never, no long\-line elision, block\-buffered
\[u2014] so an interactive run reproduces a captured one byte\-for\-byte
.SS "Execution — how it is computed"
Nothing about the answer \[u2014] only how many cores, syscalls, or elided
reads it takes to produce it.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-index\fR
scan live, ignoring any persisted index
.TP
\fB\-\-index\fR
use the persisted index when one is current
.TP
\fB\-\-mmap\fR, \fB\-\-no\-mmap\fR
accepted for rg parity; gist picks its own read strategy
.TP
\fB\-j\fR, \fB\-\-threads\fR=\fINUM\fR
cap the worker pool at this many, or 0 for gist's own topology
.sp
caps the work\-stealing worker pool at N (0 = gist's own topology), the same
bound rg's \-j sets; results are identical
.TP
\fB\-\-dfa\-size\-limit\fR, \fB\-\-regex\-size\-limit\fR=\fIVALUE\fR
accepted for rg parity; gist's engines have no such ceiling
.TP
\fB\-\-line\-buffered\fR
flush every finished line as it is found
.sp
improvement: no finished line is ever held, but every finished line already in
hand leaves in ONE write \[u2014] rg's LineWriter pays one syscall per line
for the same interactivity. The boundary is the run's real terminator, so
\-\-null\-data records flush on NUL (rg's line writer only knows \en)
.TP
\fB\-\-block\-buffered\fR
coalesce output into ramped blocks
.sp
improvement: a RAMPED block \[u2014] the first fragment is never held and the
threshold then doubles to the ceiling, so `| head \-1` answers immediately and
a closed pipe is discovered within a kilobyte; rg's BufWriter holds the first
byte as long as the last. Size it with \-\-buffer\-size (rg's 8 KiB is a
constant)
.TP
\fB\-\-buffer\-size\fR=\fISIZE\fR
size the block buffer (default 64K)
.sp
ceiling for \-\-block\-buffered, in bytes with an optional K/M suffix (default
64K, a Linux pipe's capacity); 0 restores the default. Implies
\-\-block\-buffered when no cadence was named
.SS "Configuration — read before the run"
Honored before argv parsing begins, so they can suppress what would otherwise
have supplied flags.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-config\fR
ignore the committed charter and the personal preferences file
.sp
suppresses .irregex.toml and $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/gist/preferences for this run;
GIST_NO_CONFIG=1 is the environment spelling. Read from raw argv before either
file is opened
.SH "ENVIRONMENT"
.TP
\fBGIST_DIR\fR
artifact home for the index and the atlas (default .local/gist\-verify)
.TP
\fBGIST_ROOTS\fR
the roots to index when none are given
.TP
\fBGIST_SKIP\fR
extra directories to prune from the index and freshness walks
.TP
\fBGIST_HINTS\fR
0 mutes the stderr guidance channel; results are untouched either way
.TP
\fBGIST_UNCAP\fR
1 lifts the soft output budget, as \-\-uncap does per\-query
.TP
\fBGIST_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS\fR
resize the soft output budget, in tokens
.TP
\fBGIST_MAX_OUTPUT_BYTES\fR
resize the soft output budget, in bytes
.TP
\fBGIST_TRACE\fR
comma\-separated phase lenses (amend, journal, reconcile, warm, rank, index,
query, session, fault, link) or all
.TP
\fBGIST_TRACE_FORMAT\fR
text or json; defaults to the run's own \-\-json setting
.TP
\fBGIST_NO_AUTOSERVE\fR
1 declines the automatic resident session
.TP
\fBGIST_NO_CONFIG\fR
1 ignores the committed charter and the personal preferences file
.TP
\fBNO_COLOR\fR
any value turns colorization off, as \-\-color never does
.SH "EXAMPLES"
.TP
\fBgist WalletService\fR
every occurrence in this tree, ripgrep's default shape
.TP
\fBgist 'pgxpool\e.\ew+' \-\-rank\fR
definitions first, call sites after, generated files demoted
.TP
\fBgist \-P '(?<=func )\ew+' services\fR
a lookbehind, still trigram\-prefiltered
.TP
\fBgist \-t zig \-l 'fn write'\fR
just the Zig files that contain it
.TP
\fBgist index && gist status\fR
persist the index, then check what it covers
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
\fB0\fR
the search matched, or the verb succeeded
.TP
\fB1\fR
the search ran and matched nothing
.TP
\fB2\fR
a usage, parse, path, or unsupported\-flag error
.SH "SEE ALSO"
relate(1), irregex(1), rg(1), grep(1)
